#{[{:activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186230044} :activity/id #uuid "e1f24137-95a9-5d07-991d-2ce3ba7ada0d" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049146 :topic/id #uuid "de6ce0f8-a513-5c11-b2ac-9d3484be570b" :topic/math-academy-id 6679} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186230044 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186230177 :tutorial/content "Consider the first-order inhomogeneous ODE with constant coefficients given by\n\n$$\nay' + by = f(x),\n$$\n\nwhere $a$ and $b$ are constants.\n\nAs we know, the method of finding the general solution is to find the sum of the complementary solution $y_c(x)$ and a particular solution $y_p(x).$\n\n$$\ny(x) = y_c(x) + y_p(x)\n$$\n\nTo find the particular solution $y_p(x),$ we examine the functional form of the forcing term $f(x)$ and guess a form for $y_p(x)$ that matches it. \n\nAn ODE exhibits **exponential forcing** if the forcing term $f(x)$ is an exponential function\n\n$$\nf(x) = Ke^{k x},\n$$\n\nwhere $K$ is a constant. \n\nProvided that the forcing function $e^{kx}$ is not a term in the complementary solution, our trial guess for the particular solution is an exponential function with the same exponent. That is\n\n$$\ny_p(x) = \\alpha\\,e^{k x},\n$$\n\nwhere $\\alpha$ is a constant to be determined.\n\nIn this lesson, we focus on solving first-order inhomogeneous ODEs with **exponential forcing**. Let's look at a concrete example." :tutorial/id #uuid "7a60ff4f-cb9d-522a-a94a-d16f43b6a04a" :tutorial/math-academy-id 14449 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "0804d262-6611-5c45-bc86-10e3c26e86a2" :step/math-academy-id 597439 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186230045}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186230045 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186230227 :tutorial/content "Consider the differential equation \n\n$$\n\\frac {\\textrm{d} y} {\\textrm{d} x} + 3 y = 4 e ^ {2x}.\n$$\n\nThe associated homogeneous equation is\n\n$$\n\\frac {\\textrm{d} y} {\\textrm{d} x} + 3 y = 0\n$$\n\nand therefore, the complementary solution is \n\n$$\ny_c(x) =  A e^{-3x},\n$$\n\n \n\nwhere $A$ is an arbitrary constant. Let's now find the **particular solution** $y_p(x)$ to this equation.\n\nSince the right-hand side of the inhomogeneous equation is the exponential function $f(x) = 4 e^{2x},$ and this is not incorporated within the complementary solution, we assume a particular solution of the form\n\n$$\ny_p(x) = \\alpha e^{2x},\n$$\n\nwhere $\\alpha$ is a constant to be determined.\n\nCalculating the first derivative of $y_p$ gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\textrm{d}y_p}{\\textrm{d}x} = 2\\alpha e ^{2x}.\n$$\n\nTo find the value of $\\alpha,$ we substitute $y_p$ and $y_p'$ into the differential equation and solve for $\\alpha{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(2\\alpha {e}^{2x})+3(\\alpha {e}^{2x}) & =4{e}^{2x} \\\\ (2+3)\\alpha {e}^{2x} & =4{e}^{2x} \\\\ 5\\alpha {e}^{2x} & =4{e}^{2x} \\\\ \\alpha  & =\\frac{4}{5}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the particular solution $y_p(x)$ is\n\n$$\ny_p(x) = \\dfrac{4}{5} e ^{2x}.\n$$\n\nFinally, the **general solution** is given by\n\n$$\ny = y_c + y_p = A e^{-3x} + \\dfrac{4}{5} e ^{2x}.\n$$\n\nWe can employ a similar strategy when the forcing function is a sum of exponential functions. Let's see an example." :tutorial/id #uuid "62cac4a5-105b-5e43-8d88-63ce73a86e94" :tutorial/math-academy-id 14700 :tutorial/title "A Worked Example"} :step/id #uuid "c3a6d6f6-44d9-519b-8417-7113691d787c" :step/math-academy-id 597440 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186230046}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186230046 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186229162 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186228896 :question/id #uuid "5e56989a-feb1-590a-8684-9442dee1b1cb" :question/math-academy-id "e-21615"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "42bcc7e2-88f7-5ef9-8c51-77c1e2c8e8af" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049370} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050271}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186229626} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186229641} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186305078} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186305080} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186305083} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186305085} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186305086} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186305087} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186305088} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186305089}] :knowledge-point/title "Finding a Particular Solution to a First-Order Inhomogeneous ODE With Exponential Forcing"} :step/id #uuid "bfa02bee-ddb6-54c2-afc0-6e67786db1f4" :step/math-academy-id 597441 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186230047}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186230047 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186230178 :tutorial/content "So far, our guesses for the particular solution have worked because the forcing term did not already appear in the complementary solution.\n\nSometimes, however, the right-hand side of the differential equation has the same form as a term that appears in the complementary solution $y_c(x).$ When this happens, our usual guess for $y_p(x)$ fails because it duplicates part of the complementary solution and cannot produce a new solution.\n\nIn this situation, we must modify our guess by *multiplying it by $x.$* Specifically, if the forcing term has the form $K e^{kx}$ and the complementary solution contains a term like $Ae^{kx},$ then instead of guessing\n\n$$\ny_p(x) = \\alpha e^{kx},\n$$\n\nwe try\n\n$$\ny_p(x) = \\alpha \\boldsymbol{x} e^{kx}.\n$$\n\nThis works because multiplying by $x$ creates a new function that is *linearly independent* of the complementary solution. As a result, our guess no longer overlaps with $y_c(x)$ and can serve as a valid particular solution. We'll discuss linear independence of functions in more detail in future lessons.\n\nLet’s see how this works in practice using a concrete example." :tutorial/id #uuid "e6655774-c9ab-58c6-bfec-5013b08b7918" :tutorial/math-academy-id 14450 :tutorial/title "Cases When the Forcing Term is Part of the Complementary Solution"} :step/id #uuid "b7dc2094-d676-591f-98e7-87919cdf8889" :step/math-academy-id 597445 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186230048}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186230048 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186230229 :tutorial/content "Consider the differential equation\n\n$$\ny' + 5y = 10e^{-5x}.\n$$\n\nThe associated homogeneous equation is\n\n$$\ny' + 5y = 0\n$$\n\nand so the complementary solution is \n\n$$\ny_c(x) = Ae^{-5x},\n$$\n\nwhere $A$ is an arbitrary constant. \n\nNotice that the forcing term is $f(x) = 10 e^{-5x},$ and yet $e^{-5x}$ is a term in the complementary solution. So, in this case, we assume a particular solution that takes the form \n\n$$\ny_p(x) = \\alpha x  e ^ {-5 x} ,\n$$\n\nwhere $\\alpha$ is a constant to be determined.\n\nWe calculate the first derivative of $y_p$ using the product rule for differentiation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{p}^{'} & =(\\alpha x{e}^{-5x})^{'} \\\\  & =\\alpha (x{e}^{-5x})^{'} \\\\  & =\\alpha ({e}^{-5x}-5x{e}^{-5x}) \\\\  & =\\alpha (1-5x){e}^{-5x}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the value of $\\alpha,$ we substitute $y_p$ and $y_p'$ into the differential equation and solve for $\\alpha.$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(\\alpha (1-5x){e}^{-5x})+5\\alpha x{e}^{-5x} & =10{e}^{-5x} \\\\ (1-5x+5x)\\alpha {e}^{-5x} & =10{e}^{-5x} \\\\ \\alpha {e}^{-5x} & =10{e}^{-5x} \\\\ \\alpha  & =10\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the particular solution $y_p(x)$ is\n\n$$\ny_p(x) = 10x  e ^ {-5x}\n$$\n\nand thus the general solution to the equation is\n\n$$\ny(x) = y_c(x) + y_p(x) = Ae^{-5x} + 10xe^{-5x}.\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "c5cb7b6d-2997-5082-a597-8a0e6e4eaf5b" :tutorial/math-academy-id 14703 :tutorial/title "A Worked Example"} :step/id #uuid "11a3780c-c51f-5b21-bfc3-9b6034b2e639" :step/math-academy-id 597446 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186230049}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186230049 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186229163 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186228902 :question/id #uuid "1ea06d66-de30-514a-817f-8749abb4ab25" :question/math-academy-id "e-21621"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "3c7ce128-42e5-5a5a-8fa2-0e897504ff9d" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048813}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186229627} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186229643} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186305094} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186305107} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186305109} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186305110} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186305120} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186305130} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186305132} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186308395} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186363739} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186420166}] :knowledge-point/title "Finding a Particular Solution When the Forcing Term is Part of the Complementary Solution"} :step/id #uuid "3bc4e70a-50ff-55b4-930f-052f89424e30" :step/math-academy-id 597447 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186230050}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186230050 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186229164 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186228897 :question/id #uuid "a63b7c7a-2565-5330-8994-54abefbdd191" :question/math-academy-id "e-21616"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "af1f1fb7-3e26-5624-ac07-0be869d2bd5e" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186229634} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186229635} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186305122} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186305141} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186305142} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186305143} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186308398} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186308401} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186308402} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186308403}] :knowledge-point/title "Finding the General Solution to a First-Order Inhomogeneous ODE With Exponential Forcing"} :step/id #uuid "5976dd4e-9c4c-50fb-83c5-3e9bcb60cd32" :step/math-academy-id 597451 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186230051}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186230051 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186229165 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186228898 :question/id #uuid "d2382def-85b2-5002-9614-692f272355c8" :question/math-academy-id "e-21617"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "570160ff-c937-57e7-bd78-702f7f018e64" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186229628} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186229649} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186305144} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186305145} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186305146} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186305147} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186305148} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186308343} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186308344} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186308345} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186308346} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186308397} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186308400} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186363742} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186363758}] :knowledge-point/title "Solving an Initial Value Problem"} :step/id #uuid "fc2e409a-3ef9-58dc-81db-f72435bd3a6c" :step/math-academy-id 597454}] :activity/title "Solving First-Order Linear ODEs With Exponential Forcing" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :activity.type/lesson} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186229250}] [{:activity/course {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186046659} :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186420169} :activity/id #uuid "bc665116-5a76-55aa-9353-754f3071777d" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049146 :topic/id #uuid "de6ce0f8-a513-5c11-b2ac-9d3484be570b" :topic/math-academy-id 6679} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186420167 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186308398 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "87aacf8e-bc15-5baf-95e2-dc84ee4735c1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "A{e}^{7x}+5x{e}^{7x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186306441} {:answer/id #uuid "a6180aa2-764b-5335-9218-b9ecd269a77d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "(5x+A)e^{7x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186420162}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186420162} :answer-field/id #uuid "ef304257-c02b-5b9a-91de-da2bbd172314" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186308275}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "cafd04c6-8f34-538e-bfbf-4314bcafa2be" :question/math-academy-id "q-333168" :question/problem "Consider the differential equation \n\n$$\n{y}^{′}-7y=5{e}^{7x}.\n$$\n\nFind the general solution to this equation given that the complementary solution is ${y}_{c}(x)=A{e}^{7x},$ where $A$ is an arbitrary constant.\n\n$y(x)=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "To find the general solution of an inhomogeneous first-order linear equation with constant coefficients on the left-hand side, we must find the sum of the complementary and particular solutions.\n\nTo find the complementary solution ${y}_{c},$ we solve the corresponding homogeneous equation, given by\n\n$$\n{y}_{c}^{′}-7{y}_{c}=0.\n$$\n\nSolving this equation, we find that the complementary solution is\n\n$$\n{y}_{c}=A{e}^{7x}.\n$$\n\nwhere $A$ is an arbitrary constant.\n\nWe now need to find the particular solution ${y}_{p}(x).$\n\nWe see that the right-hand side is $5{e}^{7x},$ and ${e}^{7x}$ is a term in the complementary solution, so we assume that a particular solution takes the form \n\n$$\n{y}_{p}=\\alpha x{e}^{7x},\n$$\n\nwhere $\\alpha $ is a constant to be determined.\n\nWe calculate the first derivative of ${y}_{p}$ using the product rule for differentiation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{p}^{′} & =(\\alpha x{e}^{7x})^{′} \\\\  & =\\alpha (x{e}^{7x})^{′} \\\\  & =\\alpha ({e}^{7x}+7x{e}^{7x}) \\\\  & =\\alpha (1+7x){e}^{7x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the value of $\\alpha ,$ we substitute ${y}_{p}$ and ${y}_{p}^{′}$ into the differential equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(\\alpha (1+7x){e}^{7x})-7\\alpha x{e}^{7x} & =5{e}^{7x} \\\\ (1+7x-7x)\\alpha {e}^{7x} & =5{e}^{7x} \\\\ \\alpha {e}^{7x} & =5{e}^{7x} \\\\ \\alpha  & =5.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the particular solution ${y}_{p}(x)$ is\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(x)=5x{e}^{7x}.\n$$\n\nNow, since the general solution is given by\n\n$$\ny(x)={y}_{c}(x)+{y}_{p}(x),\n$$\n\nthe general solution in our case is\n\n$$\ny=A{e}^{7x}+5x{e}^{7x}.\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "2c91bcfc-99f4-5bfe-804e-7364e7854038"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186420168 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186229643 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "d6d8db23-4013-5d8a-8bb0-41b6f6fa3c28" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "6xe^{2x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186420163}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186420163} :answer-field/id #uuid "9d7b7917-2ac5-5b52-a8e9-071c6e798cfb" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186228087}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "036131be-8602-586a-8ee5-fe7271608cce" :question/math-academy-id "q-332723" :question/problem "Given that the differential equation \n\n$$\n{y}^{′}-2y=6{e}^{2x},\n$$\n\nhas the complementary solution ${y}_{c}(x)=A{e}^{2x}$, find the particular solution to the equation.\n\n${y}_{p}(x)=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "To find the general solution of an inhomogeneous first-order linear equation with constant coefficients on the left-hand side, we must find the sum of the complementary and particular solutions.\n\nWe see that the right-hand side is $6{e}^{2x},$ and ${e}^{2x}$ is a term in the complementary solution, so we assume that a particular solution takes the form \n\n$$\n{y}_{p}=\\alpha x{e}^{2x},\n$$\n\nwhere $\\alpha $ is a constant that is to be determined.\n\nWe calculate the first derivative of ${y}_{p}$ using the product rule for differentiation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{p}^{′} & =(\\alpha x{e}^{2x})^{′} \\\\  & =\\alpha (x{e}^{2x})^{′} \\\\  & =\\alpha ({e}^{2x}+2x{e}^{2x}) \\\\  & =\\alpha (1+2x){e}^{2x}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the value of $\\alpha ,$ we substitute ${y}_{p}$ and ${y}_{p}^{′}$ into the differential equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(\\alpha (1+2x){e}^{2x})-2\\alpha x{e}^{2x} & =6{e}^{2x} \\\\ (1+2x-2x)\\alpha {e}^{2x} & =6{e}^{2x} \\\\ \\alpha {e}^{2x} & =6{e}^{2x} \\\\ \\alpha  & =6\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the particular solution ${y}_{p}(x)$ is\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(x)=6x{e}^{2x}.\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "748c2caa-2098-5b2d-8ce4-acf93b07faac" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186420170}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186420169 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186420166 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "f2b3766c-24fe-5e39-af92-4bf51a3a4723" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "9xe^{-6x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186420164}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186420164} :answer-field/id #uuid "f31cbcf7-0ca7-5dff-a5ff-a27c6b8e3c33" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186420165}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "388312be-28d6-5dd3-90fa-f6561f195420" :question/math-academy-id "q-332708" :question/problem "Given that the differential equation \n\n$$\n{y}^{′}+6y=9{e}^{-6x},\n$$\n\nhas the complementary solution ${y}_{c}(x)=A{e}^{-6x}$, find the particular solution to the equation.\n\n${y}_{p}(x)=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "To find the general solution of an inhomogeneous first-order linear equation with constant coefficients on the left-hand side, we must find the sum of the complementary and particular solutions.\n\nWe see that the right-hand side is $9{e}^{-6x},$ and ${e}^{-6x}$ is a term in the complementary solution, so we assume that a particular solution takes the form \n\n$$\n{y}_{p}=\\alpha x{e}^{-6x},\n$$\n\nwhere $\\alpha $ is a constant that is to be determined.\n\nWe calculate the first derivative of ${y}_{p}$ using the product rule for differentiation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{p}^{′} & =(\\alpha x{e}^{-6x})^{′} \\\\  & =\\alpha (x{e}^{-6x})^{′} \\\\  & =\\alpha ({e}^{-6x}-6x{e}^{-6x}) \\\\  & =\\alpha (1-6x){e}^{-6x}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the value of $\\alpha ,$ we substitute ${y}_{p}$ and ${y}_{p}^{′}$ into the differential equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(\\alpha (1-6x){e}^{-6x})+6\\alpha x{e}^{-6x} & =9{e}^{-6x} \\\\ (1-6x+6x)\\alpha {e}^{-6x} & =9{e}^{-6x} \\\\ \\alpha {e}^{-6x} & =9{e}^{-6x} \\\\ \\alpha  & =9\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the particular solution ${y}_{p}(x)$ is\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(x)=9x{e}^{-6x}.\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "7a3db07d-09b8-53c0-a769-ee5a0527289c" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186420168}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186420170 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186308343 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0041758a-5182-50fb-a219-5d3f3ef02024" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-{e}^{-2x}+8x{e}^{-2x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186305153} {:answer/id #uuid "55f5feee-d649-5b39-a104-bdb383b1096c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "(8x-1)e^{-2x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186420161}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186420161} :answer-field/id #uuid "19d58e7d-e179-5799-97c4-0c9c22e0553a" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186307604}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "d63971f5-c416-5771-8c44-552fcfc50a79" :question/math-academy-id "q-332936" :question/problem "Consider the following initial value problem:\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}+2y=8{e}^{-2x},\\,y(0)=-1\n$$\n\nGiven that the differential equation \n\n$$\n{y}^{′}+2y=8{e}^{-2x}\n$$\n\nhas the complementary solution ${y}_{c}=A{e}^{-2x},$ solve the initial value problem.\n\n$y(x)=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "To find the general solution of an inhomogeneous first-order linear equation with constant coefficients on the left-hand side, we must find the sum of the complementary and particular solutions.\n\nTo find the complementary solution ${y}_{c},$ we solve the corresponding homogeneous equation, given by\n\n$$\n{y}_{c}^{′}+2{y}_{c}=0.\n$$\n\nSolving this equation, we find that the complementary solution is\n\n$$\n{y}_{c}=A{e}^{-2x}\n$$\n\nwhere $A$ is an arbitrary constant.\n\nWe now need to find the particular solution ${y}_{p}(x).$\n\nWe see that the right-hand side is $8{e}^{-2x},$ and ${e}^{-2x}$ is a term in the complementary solution, so we assume a particular solution of the form\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}=\\alpha x{e}^{-2x},\n$$\n\nwhere $\\alpha $ is a constant to be determined.\n\nWe calculate the first derivative of ${y}_{p}$ using the product rule for differentiation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{p}^{′} & =(\\alpha x{e}^{-2x})^{′} \\\\  & =\\alpha (x{e}^{-2x})^{′} \\\\  & =\\alpha ({e}^{-2x}-2x{e}^{-2x}) \\\\  & =\\alpha (1-2x){e}^{-2x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the value of $\\alpha ,$ we substitute ${y}_{p}$ and ${y}_{p}^{′}$ into the differential equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(\\alpha (1-2x){e}^{-2x})+2(\\alpha x{e}^{-2x}) & =8{e}^{-2x} \\\\ (1-2x+2x)\\alpha {e}^{-2x} & =8{e}^{-2x} \\\\ \\alpha {e}^{-2x} & =8{e}^{-2x} \\\\ \\alpha  & =8.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the particular solution ${y}_{p}(x)$ is\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(x)=8x{e}^{-2x}.\n$$\n\nNow, since the general solution is given by\n\n$$\ny(x)={y}_{c}(x)+{y}_{p}(x),\n$$\n\nthe general solution in our case is\n\n$$\ny=A{e}^{-2x}+8x{e}^{-2x}.\n$$\n\nWe can find the constant $A$ using the initial conditions.\n\nSubstituting $y(0)=-1$ into the general solution gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A+0 & =-1 \\\\ A & =-1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution to the initial value problem is\n\n$$\ny=-{e}^{-2x}+8x{e}^{-2x}.\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "adfad238-f91c-58f5-8dc5-b475c8a368d7" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186420171}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186420171 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186308345 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "ca491ec8-db7c-5b57-b504-cd04e9792609" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{e}^{5x}+15x{e}^{5x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186307030} {:answer/id #uuid "3e468feb-d4ae-5e37-bcbd-d076dac5bb84" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "e^{5x}+15xe^{5x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186420160}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186420160} :answer-field/id #uuid "416fb058-4b84-52e5-8668-5c9c862047fa" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186307722}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "ceb57bf0-d702-511f-a8e6-4fe4eee1bf79" :question/math-academy-id "q-332938" :question/problem "Consider the following initial value problem:\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}-5y=15{e}^{5x},\\,y(0)=1\n$$\n\nGiven that the differential equation \n\n$$\n{y}^{′}-5y=15{e}^{5x}\n$$\n\nhas the complementary solution ${y}_{c}=A{e}^{5x},$ solve the initial value problem.\n\n$y(x)=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "To find the general solution of an inhomogeneous first-order linear equation with constant coefficients on the left-hand side, we must find the sum of the complementary and particular solutions.\n\nTo find the complementary solution ${y}_{c},$ we solve the corresponding homogeneous equation, given by\n\n$$\n{y}_{c}^{′}-5{y}_{c}=0.\n$$\n\nSolving this equation, we find that the complementary solution is\n\n$$\n{y}_{c}=A{e}^{5x}\n$$\n\nwhere $A$ is an arbitrary constant.\n\nWe now need to find the particular solution ${y}_{p}(x).$\n\nWe see that the right-hand side is $15{e}^{5x},$ and ${e}^{5x}$ is a term in the complementary solution, so we assume a particular solution of the form\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}=\\alpha x{e}^{5x},\n$$\n\nwhere $\\alpha $ is a constant to be determined.\n\nWe calculate the first derivative of ${y}_{p}$ using the product rule for differentiation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{p}^{′} & =(\\alpha x{e}^{5x})^{′} \\\\  & =\\alpha (x{e}^{5x})^{′} \\\\  & =\\alpha ({e}^{5x}+5x{e}^{5x}) \\\\  & =\\alpha (1+5x){e}^{5x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the value of $\\alpha ,$ we substitute ${y}_{p}$ and ${y}_{p}^{′}$ into the differential equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(\\alpha (1+5x){e}^{5x})-5(\\alpha x{e}^{5x}) & =15{e}^{5x} \\\\ (1+5x-5x)\\alpha {e}^{5x} & =15{e}^{5x} \\\\ \\alpha {e}^{5x} & =15{e}^{5x} \\\\ \\alpha  & =15.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the particular solution ${y}_{p}(x)$ is\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(x)=15x{e}^{5x}.\n$$\n\nNow, since the general solution is given by\n\n$$\ny(x)={y}_{c}(x)+{y}_{p}(x),\n$$\n\nthe general solution in our case is\n\n$$\ny=A{e}^{5x}+15x{e}^{5x}.\n$$\n\nWe can find the constant $A$ using the initial conditions.\n\nSubstituting $y(0)=1$ into the general solution gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A+0 & =1 \\\\ A & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution to the initial value problem is\n\n$$\ny={e}^{5x}+15x{e}^{5x}.\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "c64f98e2-0858-55ea-8d34-04c204a10d14" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186420167}}] :activity/title "Solving First-Order Linear ODEs With Exponential Forcing" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :activity.type/review} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186420159}]}
